<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#chagatai-language">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chagatai language</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chaghatai Turkic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chagatai is an extinct Turkic literary language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a>. It flourished under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>, especially in the works of the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-shir-navai">Ali-Shir Nava'i</a>. It remained the main literary Turkic language of Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> for centuries and was the language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>'s memoirs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chagatai-language.jpg" alt="Chagatai language" width="320" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ThatDohDude · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chagatai_in_Nastaliq.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
